# Cloudflare `_headers` — parsed by the Worker's static-asset layer, never served.
# See docs-site/public/_headers for the full reasoning; both sites must send the
# same three headers Vercel set implicitly and Cloudflare does not set at all.

# `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` matters here too: embed.html is designed to be
# framed by other sites, and the playground is the target of every `#z=` permalink
# the README and the docs emit. `Strict-Transport-Security` matches the two-year
# max-age browsers already hold from Vercel, without `includeSubDomains` — as
# Vercel sent it.
/*
  Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *
  Content-Disposition: inline
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=63072000

# Cloudflare revalidates every asset by default; Vercel marked fingerprinted files
# immutable. Vite fingerprints everything under /assets/ (both entry bundles, the
# CodeMirror and jspdf chunks, and all 46 font files), so those URLs can never
# change identity: cache them for a year. The two HTML entries keep the
# revalidating default, which is what makes a deploy visible immediately.
/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# The embed viewer is a chrome-less <iframe> target, not a page anyone should reach
# from a search result — the canonical experience is the playground itself, and the
# plan an embed shows lives in a `#z=` fragment a crawler never receives anyway.
# `noindex, follow`, not a robots.txt Disallow: a Disallow would stop the crawler
# fetching the page at all, and a header it never reads cannot de-index anything.
# `follow` keeps the outbound links (the brand link back to the playground) live.
/embed.html
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, follow

# The playground SHELL may only be framed by us; `/embed.html` deliberately may be
# framed by anyone. That asymmetry is the whole rule. A `#z=` fragment is
# third-party content — a crafted plan compiles to an SVG whose labels can read like
# anything, including a security warning — and what turns such a drawing into a
# working deception is framing the full editor chrome inside someone else's page, so
# the surrounding address bar says something other than ours. The embed viewer is
# the surface that IS meant to sit in other people's pages (the README, blogs,
# Confluence), so it keeps no framing restriction at all, and scripts/smoke.mjs
# asserts that ABSENCE — the negative check is the contract.
#
# BOTH paths are listed because `_headers` matches the REQUEST path, not the file
# the asset layer ends up serving. Measured on a local `wrangler dev` against this
# exact config, with two probe rules carrying distinguishable values: `/` came back
# with only the `/` rule's header and `/index.html` with only the `/index.html`
# rule's — so the `/  /index.html  200` rewrite in _redirects does NOT carry
# `/index.html`'s headers to `/`. Either rule alone would leave the other path
# unprotected, and `/index.html` is a real, reachable file under
# `html_handling: "none"`.
#
# `X-Frame-Options` and the `frame-ancestors` directive say the same thing to two
# generations of browser: one that understands CSP ignores XFO, one that does not
# still honours it. No other CSP directive is set — a policy that also constrained
# scripts or styles would have to be kept in step with Vite's output by hand, and
# framing is the only thing being restricted here.
/
  X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN
  Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors 'self'

/index.html
  X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN
  Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors 'self'
